# SECTION 5: AUDIENCE INTELLIGENCE
**who_they_are:** Someone actively browsing for their next manicure look, likely mid-decision — has heard silver/chrome is trending but hasn't committed. Ranges from nail-art enthusiasts who already know their shape/finish preferences to first-timers unsure if silver "suits" them.

**what_they_want_from_this_post:** A confidence-building browse: enough variety to find "their" version of silver (chrome vs. glitter vs. French vs. subtle), plus reassurance about skin tone, nail length, and occasion fit — not just a photo dump.

**what_frustrates_them:** Competitor pages either assume total confidence (jump straight to looks with no reassurance) or bury useful decision-making info (skin tone, shape, occasion) under generic filler. Product-catalog pages (Amazon/Walmart) offer zero editorial guidance.

**top_5_pain_points:**
1. Uncertainty whether silver suits their skin tone/undertone
2. Assuming silver only works on long/coffin nails, not short or natural-length
3. Confusion over silver vs. chrome vs. metallic vs. holographic terminology
4. Not knowing which silver finish fits which occasion (office vs. party vs. wedding)
5. Fear that chrome/silver looks "too much" or costume-y rather than elegant

**exact_user_phrases:** "does silver suit everyone," "silver nails for short nails," "silver vs chrome nails," "silver nails for beginners," "silver nails office appropriate," "how long do silver chrome nails last," "silver nail inspo," "silver nails everyone," "chrome nails vs silver nails difference," "silver nails at home no salon," "silver french tip instead of white," "silver nails skin tone"

**intent_content_signals:** Responds well to organized "families" of looks (chrome/glitter/French/mixed) rather than one flat list; wants occasion tags; wants shape callouts; wants short, skimmable finish descriptions rather than dense paragraphs; photo-led but not photo-only — a sentence or two of context per idea performs better than pure captions.

